@MildTomato RE kitchen sink, since the storybook is going to be used for reference, it would reduce friction when browsing for components and their different possible states. It increases component discoverabiljty and reduces chance for duplicate components where a separate version is built in studio. 

It also helps development for components that are low on interactions, since contributors can see at a glance all the different prop permutations that might result in a bug. This is especially so since we don't have unit tests so visual checking is necessary, and not all contributors have access to the local platform dev to see all possible ways a component is used. Cuts down on human error as well, and contributors don't need to do a video demo for each change, a screenshot might suffice. 

For interaction heavy components like ContextMenu it would still be better to have a separate story file.
